[DOCUMENT]
TITLE: Fingerprints and what happens when a parent changes

[GRAMMAR]
IMPORT_FROM_FILE: @repo

[NARRATIVE]
UID: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-DIRTY-RIPPLE
TITLE: Fingerprints and what happens when a parent changes
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
WIDGET: rows
STATEMENT: >>>
Dirty edges, signing, revocation, where a dirty edge blocks, and the git hazards.

- An edge is dirty when the child's recorded fingerprint no longer matches the parent's current contract, or the parent is gone. An unsigned 0000000 is not dirty. Every fingerprint must have a matching relation, or the check fails. [WORKS]
- The detector is two Python scripts, and their walk is the ripple. Under the layer stack that becomes a semantic-layer rule per role, a handler that marks after each write, and a report on read. See [LINK: DEC-LAYER-STACK]. Feeds [LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-GAP-SEMANTICS-LIVE-IN-TOOLS-WITH-NO-OWNER]. [FALLS OUT: DEC-LAYER-STACK, L3 rule per role (a per-role field list), post handler marks, scribe.read(check) reports]
- Which fields count toward a contract is a per-project option. Changing the selection makes every signed edge suspect again. [WORKS]
- Three shapes for the ripple are on the table — a per-field flag, a typed selector tree (unmeasured), or a per-role field list with a stored hash. No decision picks one. See [LINK: SPIKE-RIPPLE-SELECTOR-AST]. [UNADDRESSED]
- Clearing a dirty edge means signing — rewriting the child's hash to the parent's current one. It is refused while the parent is unsettled. Today a human does it at pull-request review. See [LINK: DEC-FP-ACCEPT-AUTHORITY]. Speaks about [LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-TYPE-DECISION]. Feeds [LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-GAP-IDENTITY-HAS-NO-CARRIER]. [OPEN: DEC-FP-ACCEPT-AUTHORITY]
- Revocation gets a 'revoked' slot on the entry; revoking an unsigned entry, or signing over a revoked one, is refused. Open: who may revoke, what a rebase does (re-accept automatically, warn, or block), and who re-signs their own conflict. See [LINK: DEC-FP-ACCEPT-AUTHORITY]. Speaks about [LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-TYPE-DECISION]. Feeds [LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-GAP-IDENTITY-HAS-NO-CARRIER]. [OPEN: DEC-FP-ACCEPT-AUTHORITY]
- A signature pasted back by hand looks identical to a real one. Nothing proves a fingerprint change went through scribe. Feeds [LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-GAP-IDENTITY-HAS-NO-CARRIER]. [GAP]
- A dirty edge blocks only in nix flake check. Unsigned and revoked entries are tolerated everywhere. Nothing checks at commit or push. Feeds [LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-GAP-MERGE-BOUNDARY-IS-NOT-A-JOINT]. [WORKS]
- Severity by joint — legal in the canon, a warning at commit, a refusal at push and merge — has been described and not decided. Feeds [LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-GAP-MERGE-BOUNDARY-IS-NOT-A-JOINT]. [UNADDRESSED]
- Git hazards, catalogued: drift after a rebase is detected; a merge conflict inside a fingerprint field is not; rerere replays old resolutions silently; absorb can route a fingerprint hunk to the wrong commit. [WORKS]
- A contract change and every acceptance it invalidates must land in the same pull request. Feeds [LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-GAP-MERGE-BOUNDARY-IS-NOT-A-JOINT]. [WORKS]
- Collapsing fingerprint history before merge is dissolved — the main-lineage mechanism leaves nothing to collect. See [LINK: MECH-FP-MAIN-LINEAGE] and [LINK: MECH-FP-PREMERGE-GC]. Speaks about [LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-TYPE-MECHANISM]. [FALLS OUT: MECH-FP-MAIN-LINEAGE, nothing survives to collect]
- When a parent or relation is removed, the fingerprint entry that pointed at it should retire. No handler does that after an unrelate or delete. [GAP]
- A node that links to a file should become dirty when the file changes. No hash over a file's contents is defined. [GAP]
